[low, footgun] git-store/src/main.rs:325-365 (put dispatch) — the JSON-vs-name heuristic misfires in both directions. (1) An entity legitimately named 'true', 'null', '42', or '[]' parses as JSON, so the old form 'put recipe 42' silently becomes a pure compile of the literal 42 instead of a named write — those names are unreachable through the old form. (2) An *intended* inline JSON value with a syntax error (e.g. an unquoted string) falls through to put_named: if the malformed text happens to be a valid RefPath, the CLI silently creates an entity with that garbage name and reads the value from stdin/editor. The compat path is documented as hidden, but a guard would help: e.g. only treat the token as a name when it is a valid RefPath AND does not start with one of {["0-9tfn-, or warn to stderr when falling back.
